                    'en' => [
                        'name' => 'Ali ibn Abi Talib',
                        'full_name' => 'Ali ibn Abi Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib al-Hashimi al-Qurashi',
                        'kunya' => 'Abul-Hasan',
                        'laqab' => "Amir al-Mu'minin, Abu Turab",
                        'biography' => "Ali ibn Abi Talib, the fourth Rashidun caliph, was the Prophet's cousin, husband of his daughter Fatimah az-Zahra, and father of al-Hasan and al-Husayn, the masters of the youth of Paradise. Born in Mecca ten years before the prophethood and raised in the Prophet's own household, he was the first boy to embrace Islam, at about ten years of age, and never once prostrated to an idol. On the night of the Hijrah he slept in the Prophet's bed, offering his life as ransom, then returned the trusts held by the Prophet to their owners before migrating himself. He fought at Badr, Uhud, the Trench, Khaybar and every campaign except Tabuk, when the Prophet left him in charge of Medina, telling him: 'Are you not pleased to be to me as Harun was to Musa, except that there is no prophet after me?' At Khaybar the Prophet gave him the banner, declaring: 'Tomorrow I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love' — and victory came at his hands. Umar said, 'Ali is the best judge among us'; the three caliphs before him relied on his counsel in law and judgement. Elected caliph after Uthman's martyrdom in 35 AH, he bore immense civil strife with patience and moved the capital to Kufa. He was struck down by the Kharijite Ibn Muljam while calling people to the dawn prayer in Ramadan 40 AH, sealing the era of the rightly-guided caliphate.",
                        'virtues' => "One of the ten promised Paradise and the first youth to accept Islam. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'You are from me and I am from you,' and 'Whoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla.' Conqueror of Khaybar, husband of Fatimah, famed for courage, asceticism, eloquence and mastery of judgement.",
                        'famous_narrations' => "He narrated 536 hadiths, including the Prophet's words to him at Khaybar: 'By Allah, that Allah should guide one man through you is better for you than red camels.'",
                        'martyrdom' => 'Ali was struck on the head with a poisoned sword by the Kharijite Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muljam at dawn on 19 Ramadan 40 AH, as he roused the people for Fajr at the mosque of Kufa. He lingered two days, counselling his sons to fear Allah and hold to the community, and died on the night of 21 Ramadan at the age of sixty-three. With his martyrdom the thirty years of prophetic caliphate foretold by the Prophet ﷺ came to a close.',
