                        'biography' => "Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib was the Prophet's uncle and foster-brother, the Master of Martyrs and the Lion of Allah and of His Messenger. Born in Mecca two to four years before the Prophet ﷺ, he grew into one of the bravest horsemen and hunters of Quraysh. His conversion in the second year of the prophethood was a turning point: returning from a hunt, he learned that Abu Jahl had insulted and abused the Prophet, so he strode into the sacred mosque, struck Abu Jahl with his bow and declared, 'Do you insult him when I follow his religion?' He remained firm in Islam and became one of its strongest defenders. After migrating to Medina, he carried the first banner the Prophet ever tied in Islam, in the expedition of Sif al-Bahr. At Badr he fought heroically, slaying the Qurayshi champion Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah in single combat, marked in battle by an ostrich feather. At Uhud in Shawwal 3 AH he fought ferociously, cutting down more than thirty men, until Wahshi the Abyssinian struck him with a javelin while he fought, and he fell a martyr. The Prophet ﷺ stood over his mutilated body and grieved as he never grieved, saying 'I shall never be afflicted with a loss like yours,' and named him the Master of Martyrs. He rests with the martyrs of Uhud, where his grave is known to this day.",
                        'virtues' => "Uncle and foster-brother of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: 'The master of martyrs is Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib.' Titled the Lion of Allah and of His Messenger, his conversion gave the Muslims of Mecca strength and protection, and he bore the first banner tied in Islam.",
