                        'biography' => "Khalid ibn al-Walid is the most celebrated military commander in Islamic history, named by the Prophet ﷺ 'a sword among the swords of Allah, drawn against the polytheists.' Born in Mecca into Banu Makhzum — the clan entrusted with Quraysh's cavalry — he was bred to horsemanship and the arts of war. Before his Islam he commanded the Qurayshi cavalry at Uhud, turning the tide against the Muslims. He embraced Islam in Safar 8 AH alongside Amr ibn al-As, and the Prophet told him: 'Praise be to Allah who has guided you.' That same year at Mu'tah, after the three appointed commanders fell as martyrs, Khalid seized the banner and extricated the army — the day the Prophet announced that 'a sword among the swords of Allah' had taken command. He witnessed the Conquest of Mecca, Hunayn and the siege of Taif, and demolished the idol al-Uzza. In the Ridda wars he was Abu Bakr's strong arm, defeating Tulayhah at Buzakhah and Musaylimah the Liar at Yamama. He then led the conquest of Iraq, made his legendary desert march across the Samawah wastes to Syria, won at Ajnadayn, and commanded the decisive victory at Yarmouk in 15 AH. He was never defeated in battle, before or after Islam. He died in his bed at Homs in 21 AH, lamenting: 'I have fought in a hundred battles or near it, and there is not a hand-span of my body without the mark of a sword, a spear or an arrow — yet here I die on my bed as a camel dies. May the eyes of cowards never sleep.'",
