                        'biography' => "Bilal ibn Rabah, the muezzin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, is one of Islam's earliest believers and its most enduring symbol of steadfast faith under persecution. Born in Mecca to parents of Abyssinian origin, he was a slave of Umayyah ibn Khalaf. He embraced Islam early — the seventh person to do so — and when his master learned of it, he tortured him mercilessly, dragging him onto the scorching sands of Mecca at noon and pressing a great boulder onto his chest, demanding he renounce Muhammad. Bilal's only answer was 'Ahad, Ahad' — One, One. Abu Bakr bought him and freed him for the sake of Allah. After migrating to Medina, when the call to prayer was instituted, the Prophet ﷺ chose Bilal as the first muezzin of Islam for his resonant voice; he also served as keeper of the Prophet's treasury. He fought at Badr — where his former tormentor Umayyah ibn Khalaf was slain — and at Uhud, the Trench and every campaign. On the day of the Conquest of Mecca, the Prophet commanded him to climb atop the Kaaba itself and raise the adhan, sealing the end of the age of oppression. The Prophet ﷺ told him: 'I heard the patter of your sandals before me in Paradise.' After the Prophet's death Bilal could not bear to remain in Medina and left for Syria as a fighter, giving the adhan only once more — and reducing all Medina to tears. He died in Damascus in 20 AH, saying: 'Tomorrow we meet the beloved ones — Muhammad and his companions.'",
                        'virtues' => "Seventh person to embrace Islam and its first muezzin. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'I heard the patter of your sandals before me in Paradise.' He endured torture upon the burning sands proclaiming 'Ahad, Ahad,' and Umar would say: 'Abu Bakr is our master, and he freed our master.'",
