                        'biography' => "Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Mother of the Believers, was the first wife of the Prophet ﷺ and the very first of all creation to believe in his message. Born in Mecca into the Banu Asad clan of Quraysh about fifteen years before the Year of the Elephant, she was known even in the pre-Islamic era as At-Tahirah — the Pure — and was a wealthy merchant who employed men to trade on her behalf. She engaged the young Muhammad ﷺ to lead her caravan to Syria, and impressed by his honesty and the wonders her servant Maysarah reported, she proposed marriage; he was twenty-five, she — by the famous account — forty. She bore him al-Qasim, Abdullah, Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthum and Fatimah, and he took no other wife while she lived. When revelation first descended upon him in the cave of Hira and he returned trembling, crying 'Cover me, cover me,' it was Khadijah who steadied him with immortal words: 'Never, by Allah! Allah will never disgrace you — you keep the ties of kinship, bear others' burdens, give to the destitute, honor the guest and stand by every just cause.' She supported him with her person and her wealth through the first years of persecution and the boycott of the ravine. Allah sent her greetings of peace through Jibril and glad tidings of a house in Paradise of hollowed pearl, free of clamor and fatigue. She died three years before the Hijrah, in what became known as the Year of Sorrow, and the Prophet ﷺ never ceased to honor her memory: 'I was granted her love.'",
