                        'biography' => "Aisha bint Abi Bakr, Mother of the Believers, was the most beloved wife of the Prophet ﷺ and the most learned woman of this nation. Born in Mecca in the household of as-Siddiq about four or five years after the start of the prophethood, she never knew a day outside Islam. The Prophet ﷺ married her in Mecca and the marriage was consummated in Medina; she was the only virgin he married, and revelation would descend upon him while he was under her blanket — a distinction shared by no other wife. Asked who was dearest to him of all people, he answered: 'Aisha'; and of men? 'Her father.' When the slanderers of al-Ifk accused her falsely, Allah revealed her innocence from above the seven heavens in verses of Surah an-Nur recited until the Day of Judgement. She was a marvel of intelligence, memory and jurisprudence. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said: 'Never did we companions find a hadith difficult and ask Aisha except that we found knowledge of it with her.' She narrated 2,210 hadiths, the senior companions consulted her on inheritance law and juristic verdicts, and the great jurists of the next generation — Urwah ibn az-Zubayr, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad — were her students. The Prophet ﷺ died in her room, on her day, resting against her chest, and was buried in her chamber. She taught the nation for some forty-eight years after him and died in Medina in 58 AH, buried in al-Baqi.",
