                    'en' => [
                        'name' => 'Fatimah az-Zahra',
                        'full_name' => 'Fatimah bint Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ',
                        'kunya' => 'Umm Abiha (Mother of her Father)',
                        'laqab' => 'Az-Zahra, Leader of the Women of Paradise',
                        'biography' => "Fatimah az-Zahra, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the most beloved of his daughters, is the leader of the women of Paradise. Born in Mecca about five years before the prophethood, while Quraysh were rebuilding the Kaaba, she was the youngest of the Prophet's daughters by Khadijah. She lived the years of persecution at her father's side: when the idolaters threw camel entrails upon his back as he prostrated at the Kaaba, it was the young Fatimah who removed them and rebuked the offenders. Her tender care for her father earned her the title Umm Abiha — 'mother of her father.' After migrating to Medina she married Ali ibn Abi Talib in 2 AH; their household was the most austere and most blessed in Islam, and she bore al-Hasan and al-Husayn — the masters of the youth of Paradise — as well as Zaynab and Umm Kulthum. When she entered upon the Prophet ﷺ he would rise, kiss her, and seat her in his place. He said: 'Fatimah is a part of me; whoever angers her angers me.' When she complained of the toil of the grinding-stone and asked for a servant, he taught her instead the glorification of Allah after every prayer and before sleep — 'better for you than a servant.' In his final illness he confided to her that he would be taken, and she wept; then that she would be the first of his family to join him and the leader of the women of Paradise, and she laughed. She died six months after him, in Ramadan 11 AH, about twenty-nine years old, and was buried at night in al-Baqi as she had wished.",
                        'virtues' => "The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Fatimah is the leader of the women of the people of Paradise,' and 'Fatimah is a part of me; what troubles her troubles me.' Of all people she most resembled the Prophet in bearing and conduct; mother of his two grandsons.",
                        'famous_narrations' => "She narrated from her father, including his words to her: 'Are you not pleased to be the leader of the women of Paradise — or of the believing women?'",
                    ],
